Burst times with the same length can be handled together. A frequency table stores how many jobs have each burst time. Processing the burst lengths in increasing order gives the same SJF order, while the count lets the loop update several equal jobs at once.

For instance, `[2, 1, 2]` has one job of length 1 and two jobs of length 2. The waits are `0, 1, 3`, so the result is `4 // 3 = 1`.

```python
class Solution:
    def solve(self, bt):
        if not bt:
            return 0

        counts = {}
        for burst in bt:
            counts[burst] = counts.get(burst, 0) + 1

        elapsed = 0
        waiting_sum = 0

        for burst in sorted(counts):
            amount = counts[burst]
            waiting_sum += amount * elapsed
            elapsed += amount * burst

        return waiting_sum // len(bt)
```

Time complexity: `O(n + k log k)`, where `k` is the number of different burst times.
Space complexity: `O(k)` for the frequency table. The input list is not modified.

An integer result is returned, so any fractional part of the average is discarded.

All jobs are available at the same time, so a min-heap can repeatedly choose the smallest burst time. After removing one job, the current elapsed time is its waiting time for the next job.

This avoids changing the input array, but the heap still needs extra memory. If there are no jobs, the answer is zero.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public long solve(int[] bt) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> jobs = new PriorityQueue<>();
        for (int burst : bt) {
            jobs.offer(burst);
        }

        long elapsed = 0;
        long waitingSum = 0;

        while (!jobs.isEmpty()) {
            waitingSum += elapsed;
            elapsed += jobs.poll();
        }

        if (bt.length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }
        return waitingSum / bt.length;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` because every heap insertion and removal takes logarithmic time.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the priority queue. The input array stays unchanged.

Sorting the burst times puts the shortest jobs first. The first job waits for 0 time, and every next job waits for the total burst time of all jobs before it. I add each current total to the waiting sum, then divide by the number of jobs.

For example, burst times `[4, 3, 7, 1]` become `[1, 3, 4, 7]`. The waiting times are `0, 1, 4, 8`, so the integer average is `13 / 4 = 3`.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    long long solve(vector<int>& bt) {
        sort(bt.begin(), bt.end());

        long long elapsed = 0;
        long long waitingSum = 0;

        for (int burst : bt) {
            waitingSum += elapsed;
            elapsed += burst;
        }

        if (bt.empty()) {
            return 0;
        }
        return waitingSum / bt.size();
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n log n)` for sorting.
Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the sorting space. The input array is changed because it is sorted.

A direct simulation also works: find the smallest unfinished job, add the current elapsed time to the waiting sum, and mark that job as used. This keeps the scheduling rule visible in the code and does not reorder `bt`.

The `used` array handles duplicate burst times correctly because each position still represents a separate job. Empty input is handled before the division.

```cpp
#include <climits>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
  public:
    long long solve(vector<int>& bt) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(bt.size());
        if (n == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        vector<bool> used(n, false);
        long long elapsed = 0;
        long long waitingSum = 0;

        for (int completed = 0; completed < n; ++completed) {
            int nextJob = -1;
            for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
                if (!used[i] &&
                    (nextJob == -1 || bt[i] < bt[nextJob])) {
                    nextJob = i;
                }
            }

            used[nextJob] = true;
            waitingSum += elapsed;
            elapsed += bt[nextJob];
        }

        return waitingSum / n;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n^2)` because the next shortest unfinished job is searched each time.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the used flags. The input array is left unchanged.
